A||||47||batboy||More clutch problems||||8||
Z||000000||batboy||07-09-2000||10:26 AM||||The clutch pedal on my 85 GT started feeling hard to push in during the last couple of inches of travel and now it won't completely disengage the clutch with it fully depressed (which is depressing). At the time it happened, I had just pulled in off the highway and the clutch disc itself was not slipping then. I've already done the pedal replacement from aluminum to steel. Sounds like hydraulic failure to me...what's everyone else think???||||reg||8
Z||000001||Patrick||07-09-2000||03:03 PM||||<BR>I dunno batboy, my experience with clutch hydraulic problems is that the pedal is <B><I>easier</I></B> to depress (and nothing happens), rather than the pedal being <B><I>harder</I></B> to depress...||||reg||5
Z||000002||ROB MAC||07-09-2000||05:11 PM||||When I did the replacement I rebuilt the master cylinder. After about a week it blew out the slave. Yours sounds like the it is binding up after the slave. The fact that it is hard to depress seems to indicate it is holding pressure. Any leaks?||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||07-09-2000||05:31 PM||||No leaks at all. The hydraulics do seem to work, a little, just not enough to disengage completely. It gets a bit harder during the last couple inches before I hit the floor. Maybe I'll take the pedal assembly apart again. That master cylinder rod banjo did have some wear on it, but I thought my new Rodney bushing fixed that. Maybe something else is wore out. It does act a lot like when I had the bent aluminum pedal. It's not the slave bracket flexing, I also checked that. Later this evening when it cools down a little and get some shade on the driveway, I'll do some more troubleshooting. IT'S HOT OUT TODAY!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||07-09-2000||06:03 PM||||<BR>Of course, it could feel harder towards the bottom of the pedal travel if there was air in the system. The first part of the travel would simply be the air being compressed, and the rest of the travel would require more pressure because the clutch is actually starting to be disengaged.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||07-09-2000||10:59 PM||||It could be the throw out bearing binding on the shaft.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||07-09-2000||11:24 PM||||Carrolles...bite your tongue...I don't want it to be the throw-out bearing. Actually, there doesn't seem to be any binding. Ok, after messing around some more, here is more info. It's not like it's really hard to push that last couple inches, it's more of a weird feeling. The pedal assembly appears to be ok, except there's about a 1/2 inch of free play as you first press the pedal in. Looks like the master cylinder rod is too short. Maybe it's worn out, but there's always been a little play in the top of the pedal. Of course it was fun to lay upside down under the dash for an hour in this heat to make you feel dizzy. After coming back in, I searched for more info and I found this written by Ed Parks. Looks like some great info.<P> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||07-10-2000||12:01 AM||||The spring in the master cylinder may be broke. It happened on my car and made the clutch feel really strange- lots of free play at the top and stiffer at the bottom because I was having to compress all three parts of the broken spring at the end of the pedal's travel.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowCG||07-11-2000||11:36 AM||||It does sound like there's air in there somewhere,or the spring thing maybe.If you want a deal on a complete new clutch assy(Borg&Beck/Luk)for a 4spd,I've got one that I can't return,and will sell it real cheap if you're interested.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||07-26-2000||09:38 PM||||LowCG, I'm still hopefull this is a hydraulic problem, but if not, I'll keep your clutch offer in mind.<P>Here's the update. I ordered a brand new clutch master cylinder (damn expensive too) after following Ed Park's and other's troubleshooting advice. After removing the old one, I was shocked at the amount of black sludge in the bottom of the reservoir. Also, the push rod banjo looks very worn. After I installed the new one, I went from about 1" of play at the top of the pedal to only about 1/2" of play.<P>I'm done working on it for the night, but tomorrow I'll be ready to bleed the system. I'm going to try the gravity method I think. I know the general proceedures, but for those that are experienced, how high does the front need to be jacked up? How  much time does it take before you need to add brake fluid to keep it from running out and having to start over again? Thanks in advance.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||07-26-2000||10:21 PM||||BB: I have found it works just as well(maybe better???)to just leve the front end on the ground. Take the lid off the master cylinder, and open up the bleeder screw on the slave, and let it drip all over. I just fill the master <B>to the top</B>, walk away for a few minutes, come back and top it off. Since you just replaced the master, might take 30 minutes of this before you get solid pedal feel. But gravity bleeding has by far, worked the best for me.||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||07-27-2000||12:02 AM||||Not jacking it up worked for you? Everyone else has told me to jack up the front. Hopefully, I don't have too much air in the system, but I realize it's a long ways to the back of the car. I CAREFULLY bench bleed it first. NEVER push the rod in very far when doing this or you can cause damage. I also capped the line as quick as I could, not more than 2 or 3 drops escaped. Changing the clutch master cylinder was easy, it's the bleeding that's worrying me, especially after hearing about all the nightmares others have had. BTW, my mechanic buddy says that if you're getting air into the system, but aren't losing fluid, it's often the M/C seals that are bad. Just a heads up for others with this problem.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Indiana_resto_guy||07-27-2000||12:31 AM||||Hey Batboy!<BR>The easiest and fastest way to bleed the clutch is to get a piece of tubing (clear) open the slave, slip an end over it and run the hose back to the master. Keep your eye on the fluid level in the master (keep it full) in the begining and start pumping. Once you get it going watch the clear tube till you see no more air and your done.||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||07-27-2000||09:13 PM||||Well, I can't get the slave bleeder screw out and managed to ruin it by rounding the shoulders off, even using a 6 point socket. I now have it soaking in penetrating oil. Before I take vice-grips to it, I'm going to buy a new one. You can buy these separately, right?||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||07-27-2000||09:48 PM||||Yes||||reg||1
Z||000015||EdsB52||07-28-2000||02:59 AM||||Hey Batboy, here's something else you don't want to hear. I bought an 84 in cherry condition, except for the fact that the clutch would not disengage. I did the obvious and installed a steel clutch pedal. Same problem. I then pulled the trans. and clutch assy to find a nearly new clutch disk and pressure plate. Further investigation revealed the pressure plate had been welded on during the reman. process and weakened a couple of fingers at that area, thus creating a constant drag and dissallowing clutch disengagement. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||07-28-2000||08:40 AM||||Bats,<P>we're you one of the ones that played with alternate oil in the clutch? (I don't have but a minute to read right now.....) If so you may still have had some contamination in the lines. You could have a rubber line bad.||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||07-28-2000||11:49 AM||||Ogre, no I did not switch, it has always had only brake fluid in the system. The only other clutch problems I've had with this 85 GT was a bent pedal, which I replaced.<P>I'm having trouble locating a bleeder valve for the slave unit. Question: do the caliper bleeder screws have the same threads as the slave? I know the hex head is a different size. I have extra calipers. If not, then maybe I need to visit the local boneyard tomorrow.<P>This sucks, everytime I read about other members having trouble with their clutch systems, I pleaded with the Fiero Deities to spare me. Must not of deflowered enough maidens...or is that sacrificed enough virgins...I get confused.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Richard Parnell||07-28-2000||03:12 PM||||Batboy-  Go to a bone yard and take a bleeder screw out of a junker.  I have a bunch of master and slave cyl around my place from trying different brands trying to get my "87 to shift.  If you change slaves be sure the replacement has the same volume as the OEM, if the volume is larger it will not throw the rod out enough to disengage the clutch.  I also recently found out that quite a few of the '90s Camaros use the same master cyl as the Fiero.  The master is easy to find and replace - finding the proper slave is the killer.  I have 4 different types that I have been sold by different auto stores.  Also gravity bleeding with the bleeder screw all the way out works best for me. That is to get the initial bunch of air out after replacing either master of slave, just be sure the reservoir doesn't go empty on you. But I still follow up with the old routine of having some one pump up the clutch (15-20 pumps) and slightly cracking the bleeder.  I repeat this 4-5 times. ||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||07-28-2000||07:48 PM||||I've heard that pumping the pedal breaks up the big air bubbles into lots of little one, which makes it harder to bleed. Since I've never done this before I'm going to follow Ed Parks instructions to the letter, at least the first time. He's the MAN.<P>NAPA auto parts told me they have lots of bleeder valves, but I'll have to bring my old one in to match it up. If the salvage yard don't have a good one, then that's what I'll do. Although most of the Fieros in the local boneyard were autos, if I remember right.<P>Can't do anything tonight because of the rain. Keep your fingers crossed that I can get it fixed tomorrow. Sure hope the bleeder screw don't break off in the slave...||||reg||1
Z||000020||SCCA FIERO||07-28-2000||08:31 PM||||I destroyed my bleeder screw too. No luck at the usual places (autozone and Checker). Hope you have better luck than me.<P>I also had the same problem as you. My clutch got hard to push in and finally stuck... master had so much crud in it it locked up.<P>The bleeding process has worked both ways for me. Was a pain level and with the car jacked up. I finally got it after a huge bottle of brake fluid had passed through the lines. I used a clear tube on the end of the bleeder and watched as the air seemed to never end. I finally got it to where not many bubbles were coming out and called it good. Been working fine ever since.<P>I think 3 cycles of the clutch pedal is as far as I would go before filling the master again.||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||07-29-2000||02:40 PM||||Blankity-blank, rassafrassa, $#!&, insert your favorite profanity here. I'm so pissed at this 85 GT that I could send it to the crusher right now in a heartbeat. I spent an hour sweating my buttg off in the sun this morning trying to remove the bleeder from the slave. Guess what? I now have to buy a new slave unit. Of course I had to order it, so it won't be in until Monday or Tuesday, plus another $75 down the money pit drain. After all this down time and money spent on the clutch hydraulics, it still might not work. Oh, please give me the strength not to drop a match down the gas tank. Stick a fork in me, I'm done...working on it for the weekend that is.||||reg||1
Z||000022||EdsB52||07-29-2000||08:10 PM||||I'm telling you Batboy, the pressure plate is dragging. You know what they say, 'Want in one hand, st!t in the other, see which fills up first.' I'm with ya bro, but just because we want the problem to be something easier, doesn't mean it's going to be easier. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000023||EdsB52||07-29-2000||08:12 PM||||That's 'sh!t. lol||||reg||1
Z||000024||batboy||07-29-2000||08:55 PM||||I'm still hopeful it's hydraulic trouble, Ed. Just because that happened to you, don't mean it's a common problem. In fact, all the research I've done and mechanics I've talked to seem to think that's unlikely. Now the hydraulic system is a known common problem with Fieros. Regardless, as bad as the master cylinder looked, it was time to be replaced and I'm sure the slave is in the same boat. The pedal feels nice and smooth all the way to the floor now, it's not feeling funny on that last inch of travel like before, but the system still has air in it and it won't disengage the clutch all the way. I can start the car in second gear and drive it. There is no noise and no slippage. I still think it was the M/C that was bad.||||reg||1
Z||000025||Tryxalon||07-29-2000||11:26 PM||||I have a similar situation. <P>Bought a 85 2m4 that has been sitting for about a year since the clutch wouldn't work and the guy didn't want to mess with it. <P>Pulled cradle, separated engine/trans replaced oil seals, clutch, TO bearing, pressure plate and reassembled. All parts work. clutch actuation confirms not binding. (lever application - gently!) Put everything back into the car. <P>Clutch would not disengaged. Checked and found bent aluminum pedal. Replaced that. No disengagement and during bleeding developed sufficient pressure to blow master cylinder seals. Replaced that. Again try to bleed and preassure blows seals on slave cylinder. This car must have sat, clutch dry for more than one year!! <BR> Replaced the slave cylinder! (surely this *&#$%*# thing should work, now!!)  <P>I am still not getting enough "throw" to disengage clutch. Actuator arm moves maybe 5/16" and obviously needs at least 13/16"<P>I bleed it and when I apply the pedal it is okay for one or two applications then the pedal goes soft.  When I bleed it again - I get bubbles - "air in system". (yep, all new DOT 3 fluid so it's not the fluid). <P>Only one thing left: I must have leak in hydraulic line.  Course, that silly thing is hard line with two flexible ends that is all one piece.  This fabrication could take a day or two, but there is no other logical answer.<P>(I'm lucky enough to have this as a "project" or "toy" so I don't have to have rely on it for transportation, but I'm not completely happy with it at the moment).  <P>Thoughts? Anything I might have missed? <P> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tryxalon (edited 07-30-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000026||batboy||07-29-2000||11:53 PM||||Huh?||||reg||1
Z||000027||EdsB52||07-30-2000||01:17 AM||||Here's something I forgot to post as a possible problem. As we all know, there is just barely enough clutch pedal travel to disengage the clutch with a good working system. A couple of other things to look at are;<P>1) Relationship between master cylinder arm and clutch pedal pin journal.<BR>     a. pin journal can gall, creating pedal end play.<BR>     b. master cylinder arm can possess a condition of elongation at the hole in which clutch pedal pin goes through, creating end play.<P>Individually, these conditions are negligible. Accumulatively, these conditions can create gremlins.<P>2) Sounds kind of funny, but the carpet can act as interference for the clutch pedal to have full deflection to the floor.<P>The fix for the clutch pedal play is to drill  a round hole in the master cylinder arm and bush the clearance. The fix for the carpet is obvious.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Patrick||07-30-2000||02:33 AM||||<BR>It's not just the carpet, it's the friggen   thick <I><B>underlay</B></I>!!! Take it out! (Leave the carpet, take out a chunk of underlay.)||||reg||4
Z||000029||batboy||07-30-2000||08:40 AM||||I already have Rodney Dickman's banjo bushing to fix the wear at where the M/C rod connects onto the pedal. I also pulled the carpet away trying to get extra clearance. I'm glad those things were mentioned though, maybe that will help others troubleshoot their cars.<P>I suppose I could drill and tap out the broken bleeder screw in the slave, but I'd rather put a new one on instead. Another thing I'm doing is installing Rodney Dickman's reinforced steel slave bracket.<P>The early Fieros (84 and 85 for sure) had a wimpy hold down bracket that can flex and eventually crack. Anyway, this flexing, along with wear in the pedal area as previously mentioned, can combine to reduce the amount of travel.||||reg||1
Z||000030||batboy||07-30-2000||08:44 AM||||I have a question that I bet Ogre and other knowledgeable members might know the answer to. To prevent this situation in the future, can I use a tiny bit of anti-seize on the bleeder threads, or will this contaminate the hydraulic fluid???||||reg||1
Z||000031||EdsB52||07-30-2000||11:31 AM||||Not to be condescending, but another consideration to have when bleeding any hydraulic system, is to bleed any conection that might trap air. I've found that cracking the line nut at the top of the M/C first, as I'm having someone depress the clutch pedal, relieves a bunch of air that would otherwise not get bled during the normal bleeding process. I found this particularly important when installing front braided steel brake lines on motorcycles. You could pressure or gravity bleed all you wanted to, but if you didn't crack the nuts at the splitter on the lower tripple clamp, you would never get pressure. I've found the same applies to that M/C nut.||||reg||1
Z||000032||Tryxalon||07-30-2000||11:37 AM||||I just went back and edited my prior post to translate it into English. Sorry! <P>Good point on the connections. I doublechecked all before I replaced fluid. (like I say, car sat for a long while!!) <P>||||reg||1
Z||000033||batboy||07-30-2000||12:03 PM||||Ed, you are right about gravity bleeding not getting all the trapped air out of the slave. Ed Parks has a slightly different way to deal with that (see URL). Probably nothing wrong with your method, sounds like it works for you. Trouble is, I do most of my work alone, so it's sometimes hard to find an assistant when I need one.<P> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#clutch</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000034||EdsB52||07-30-2000||02:05 PM||||That's cool Batboy. There's no right way and no wrong way to do something. It's kind of like an opinion. But the fact that you have no one helping you bleed the clutch doesn't mean you can't do it yourself. I'm 6'1", which is maybe why I can do this, but I can bleed the M/C or the slave cylinder by myself from the driver's seat. The M/C is easy, but the slave requires the aid of a ratchet, swivel, and extension, and is very arduous but possible. Thanks for the web site, I added it to my favorites. See ya, Ed.||||reg||1
Z||000035||William Federle||07-30-2000||03:45 PM||||BB - have you considered the speedbleeder? I know it's mainly to allow you to bleed your brakes singlehandedly, but I think there was a post in the Fiero Forum recently about using it to bleed the clutch by yourself. Info on it is at the following address.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.speedbleeder.com/feature.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.speedbleeder.com/feature.htm</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 07-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||squid||07-30-2000||04:17 PM||||I havent read all of your clutch problem posts but you seem to have been having trouble with this for a while now. This may sound strange but if you bleed your clutch system with the same technique as your brakes then it will do exactly what your describing. Its just the opposite. Press the pedal, open the valve, slide your foot off the pedal and let it fly up, then close the valve.Do this a a couple of times. If you had been doing it the other way, youll notice an immediate improvement. ||||reg||1
Z||000037||squid||07-30-2000||04:29 PM||||I forgot to mention the clear tube in a small bottle of fluid at the slave. Just my 2 cents worth. ||||reg||1
Z||000038||GT Bastard||07-30-2000||04:34 PM||||Hahahaha... When I first saw this post my BB, I thought "Damn, you poor bastard. I'm glad my clutch works fine."  Coming home from work last night I tried to put it in 1st gear at a stop light.  The damn clutch wouldn't let go. DOH!  Had to limp it home.  ||||reg||1
Z||000039||batboy||07-30-2000||04:38 PM||||Hey, Squid. It's been awhile since I've seen you been around. Ok, I guess I did read your 4.3 update. Actually, I haven't even been able to start bleeding the clutch yet. After ordering the M/C and finally getting it on and then having rain delays, I then could not get the bleeder valve off the slave. I ended up breaking it. So now I have a new slave ordered. If it weren't for bad luck, I'd have no luck at all. Maybe it is just wishful thinking, but after all the research and troubleshooting I did, my original problem sounds like it was the M/C. During the last couple of shifts I did before it screwed up completely, I noticed the clutch pedal kind of felt "funny" on the last inch or so of travel and that I had to press it all the way tight against the floor. The next time I tried starting it, the clutch wouldn't completely disengage. Weird. There was never any leakage of hydraulic fluid either. Well, at least until I broke the slave unit, anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000040||batboy||07-30-2000||05:13 PM||||My first two Fieros were autos, this will be the last manual shift Fiero I'll ever have. Even if the M/C isn't all of the problem, it does need replaced. Look at the pic below. Hopefully you can see how that banjo hole is all worn out and elongated, especially where I have the arrow drawn. The entire bottom of the M/C reservoir was full of this black sludge crap too.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/clutchMC.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000041||batboy||07-30-2000||05:15 PM||||Dog-gone-it, Cliff, when are you going to turn the delete function back on so we can clean up our double posts?<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-30-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||squid||07-30-2000||08:27 PM||||I think its leakage past the internal seals or in the master cylinder. I'll bet youll never have another clutch actuating problem as long as you own it. And if you do, youll know exactly what to do. Youll probably realize whats happening before it goes out.  ||||reg||1
Z||000043||batboy||08-02-2000||07:16 AM||||I finlly got my new slave and after it was installed and gravity bleed, it still didn't work. Then I had my neighbor help me bleed it the conventional way and I finally go it to work. With the engine running and the clutch pushed in I was able to go through all the gears.<P>I was parked in between two of my cars, so I couldn't go anywhere right then. I picked up my tools, washed my hands, and moved the Suburban out of the way. The I hopped into the 85 GT for a test drive. ARGH!!! It was messed up again! Acted just like before.<P>This is driving me crazy. What does this mean? Do I still have air in the system? After looking at the new slave, I noticed it was different than the old one. The new one looks bigger and appears to be cast iron or something. I specifically asked the parts store to double check to see if this Wagner unit was 13/16" diameter and the book showed that it was.||||reg||1
Z||000044||Carrolles||08-02-2000||08:26 AM||||When the master cylinder quit on my 85SE v-6 I bought a re-build kit from AutoZone.  Ed Parks told me that whenever the master cylinder goes the slave will soon follow.  Guess what?  The next week the slave failed.  No re-build kits were available so I installed a new one.  I jacked the left side of the car up about 5 inches to help get the air out of the slave cylinder.  So far so good after a year and about 8000 miles.<P>BTW I wouldn't recommend returning the fluid from the slave back to the master cylinder via tubing because you will never flush out the crud.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000045||SCCA FIERO||08-02-2000||05:34 PM||||batboy, is that the correct master cylinder? I thought they used the first style for 84-85 4 cylinders. I may be wrong but I do know that one is not the one that was in my 86SE 4 speed, which is pretty much the exact same thing as your Fiero.<P>I had these problems in both of my Fieros at one time or another. I managed to get it to work in my old SE and had to fight with my current Fiero for about 3 days (just bleeding and trying to get the hydrolic system working) but finally got it working. I think you just need to get lucky.<P>BTW, have you checked the slave mount?||||reg||1
Z||000046||batboy||08-02-2000||10:02 PM||||SCCA, that's the M/C the book called for and it was the same exact one as what was on it (this is a 85 GT 4 speed with a 2.8). The slave mounting bracket is a near new Rodney Dickman heavy duty reinforced bracket.<P>Well, I went out to bleed some more and was amazed at how much air bubbled out initially. Then it slowed down to just a little bubble every once in awhile. Finally, no more air for a long time. I think to myself, I finally got it bleed. I try it, same thing, not disengaging far enough. Oh guano, I'm ready to get my gun and shoot this sunny beach. I am soooo pissed!||||reg||1
Z||000047||DRH||08-03-2000||06:58 PM||||I tried bleeding mine every way imaginable. I seemed to gain ground then turn around and lose it again. <P>Anyway... The way I finally got mine done was by using vacuum. I have one of those little brake bleeding plastic cup deals (most auto parts places sell for 5 or 6 bucks). It has a clear plastic line with a couple of adapter for bleeders that connects to a clear/white plastic cup with a lid on it. There is another fitting on the lid to hook a vacuum pump to. Since I don't have a vacuum pump, I hooked it to the vacuum fitting on the plenum and started the engine.<BR>I opened the bleeder slightly (too much and it just sucks air around the bleeder threads). It should suck some fluid and air bubbles into the line. If the master cylnder cap is tight and there are no bad seals it should quit pulling anything out once it pulls a vacuum on the whole system. I then closed the bleeder, rechecked the fluid in the master, and pushed the clutch pedal a couple of times. Repeated 3 or 4 times for good measure and every thing works great.<P><BR>||||reg||1
